NAME
       test, [ - check file types and compare values

SYNOPSIS
       test EXPRESSION
       [ EXPRESSION ]
       [[ EXPRESSION ]]   (bash only)

DESCRIPTION
       test checks a condition and sets the exit status: 0 when it is true,
       1 when it is false. It prints nothing. It is what if, while and &&
       look at in shell scripts:

           if [ -f /etc/hosts ]; then echo "found"; fi

       [ is another name for test that needs a closing ]. The brackets are
       a command, not punctuation, so they need spaces around them and around
       every operator: [ "$a" = "$b" ], never ["$a"="$b"].

       Quote variables inside [ ]: an empty or unset variable otherwise
       disappears and breaks the expression ("unary operator expected").

       bash's own [[ ]] works like [ ] but is safer and stronger: variables
       need no quotes, && and || can be used inside, == compares with a
       pattern ([[ $f == *.conf ]]) and =~ with a regular expression.

       test and [ are bash builtins (see builtins(1)); /usr/bin/test and
       /usr/bin/[ also exist as programs.

EXPRESSIONS
   Files
       -e FILE    FILE exists (any kind)
       -f FILE    exists and is a regular file
       -d FILE    exists and is a directory
       -L FILE, -h FILE
                  exists and is a symbolic link
       -b FILE    a block device;  -c FILE  a character device
       -p FILE    a named pipe;    -S FILE  a socket
       -s FILE    exists and is not empty
       -r FILE    the current user may read it
       -w FILE    the current user may write it
       -x FILE    the current user may run it (or enter the directory)
       -u FILE    has the set-user-ID bit
       -g FILE    has the set-group-ID bit
       -k FILE    has the sticky bit
       -O FILE    is owned by the current user
       -G FILE    belongs to the current user's group
       F1 -nt F2  F1 is newer than F2 (modification time)
       F1 -ot F2  F1 is older than F2
       F1 -ef F2  F1 and F2 are the same file (hard links)

   Strings
       -z STRING  the string is empty
       -n STRING  the string is not empty
       S1 = S2    the strings are equal (== works too)
       S1 != S2   the strings differ

   Numbers (whole numbers only)
       N1 -eq N2  equal            N1 -ne N2  not equal
       N1 -lt N2  less than        N1 -le N2  less or equal
       N1 -gt N2  greater than     N1 -ge N2  greater or equal

       Do not use < and > for numbers: inside [ ] they are redirections,
       and inside [[ ]] they compare as text.

   Combining
       ! EXPR     true when EXPR is false
       EXPR1 -a EXPR2, EXPR1 -o EXPR2
                  and, or (inside [ ]; prefer two tests joined with && or
                  || outside the brackets)
       \( EXPR \) grouping

   Shell variables
       -v NAME    the variable NAME is set

EXAMPLES
       Stop a script when it was not given an argument:

           if [ $# -lt 1 ]; then echo "usage: $0 FILE"; exit 1; fi

       Act only on directories:

           for d in /home/*; do [ -d "$d" ] && echo "$d"; done

       Compare a user's input:

           if [ "$1" = "start" ]; then systemctl start httpd; fi

       Make a test that a checker can run:

           test -x /usr/local/bin/backup.sh && echo ok

       Check a number from a command:

           if [ "$(wc -l < /root/list.txt)" -gt 10 ]; then echo "long list"; fi

       Pattern match with bash's [[ ]]:

           [[ $HOSTNAME == server* ]] && echo "a lab server"

SEE ALSO
       builtins(1), bash(1), expr(1)
